 "productionNotes": "No single German total is agreed, so none is asserted. BMW's fiftieth-anniversary press material states 161,728 cars, repeated by Wikipedia, the Museo Nazionale dell'Automobile and Goodwood. BMW Group Classic's own page rounds to 161,000 and Hagerty uses the same figure; BMW Blog gives 161,360. The 368-car gap is unexplained by any source consulted here. The launch chronology is equally unsettled: BMW Group Classic dates the start to March 1955, BMW Blog and RM Sotheby's to April 1955, and the 300's arrival is variously December 1955 (BMW Blog), February 1956 (Wikipedia), 1956 (BMW press) or the same year as the 250 (BMW Group Classic). First-year output is 12,911 cars per BMW's press office and 'more than 10,000' per BMW Group Classic, with car number 10,000 recorded on 20 November 1955. Peak year 1957 at roughly 40,000; production ended in May 1962. No credible 250/300 split was found. Iso's own output was around a thousand cars. Hagerty puts worldwide production across seven licensees at 202,728, roughly 41,000 outside Germany, and estimates at least a quarter survive. For the BMW 600, Wikipedia and Below the Radar state 34,813 while Lane Motor Museum gives 34,318, with Wikipedia's own text saying 'about 35,000'. Below the Radar records around 27,000 of them in 1958 alone, and only about a dozen sold in Britain.",
